## Vercel Web Analytics Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for this Next.js project.

### Changes Made

**1. Installed Package**
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` package using npm
- Updated `package.json` and `package-lock.json` with the new dependency

**2. Added Analytics Component**
- Modified `src/app/layout.tsx` to import and include the Analytics component
- Added import: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";`
- Added component to the root layout body, placed before SpeedInsights component

### Implementation Details

Following the official Vercel documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), the implementation used the Next.js App Router pattern by:

1. Importing the Analytics component from `@vercel/analytics/next`
2. Adding `<Analytics />` component to the root layout's body section
3. Positioning it alongside the existing SpeedInsights component for consistency

The Analytics component is a zero-config client component that automatically tracks page views and other analytics events when deployed to Vercel.

### Verification Steps Completed

✅ Build completed successfully (`npm run build`)
✅ All 539 tests passed (`npm run test`)
✅ Linter executed (no new errors introduced)
✅ Lock file updated with new dependency

### Files Modified

- `package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency tree
- `src/app/layout.tsx` - Added Analytics import and component

### Next Steps

To enable analytics data collection:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard by navigating to the Analytics section and clicking "Enable"
3. Analytics data will begin collecting automatically after the next deployment

### Notes

- The existing custom analytics components (AnalyticsBeacon and AnalyticsConsent) remain in place for Google Analytics integration
- Vercel Web Analytics works alongside these existing analytics solutions
- The Analytics component renders no visible markup and doesn't impact static rendering
